From: well0nez <8986239+well0nez@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: iommu/sun50i, cedrus: release the VE's IOMMU ports once its engine has stopped

A damaged H.265 stream can send the video engine to an unmapped address.
The IOMMU reports the fault and resets the faulting master's port at
once, in its interrupt handler - while the engine is still running. The
engine keeps reading, the port is blocked again, and the engine stays
dead: the job times out, the next jobs' bit reader times out in setup
("timed out waiting to skip bits", -EINVAL), and every later
inter-predicted picture of every codec fails until a reboot (B6 of the
media side project), while intra pictures still decode. The engine
reset, runtime suspend with the VE power domain off, reloading the
module - none of it helps, because none of it touches the port.

Measured on the HY310, 14.10.2026 (arbeit/medien/B6-mythos):

- With the engine idle, one more pulse of the engine's two master bits
  in IOMMU_RESET_REG (a single 32-bit write pair from user space, nothing
  else) brought a dead engine back every time: the probe decoded 0/300
  before and 300/300 after, twice. The fault's display in
  IOMMU_L1PG_INT_REG and IOMMU_INT_ERR_ADDR_L1_REG stays set through it
  and through the recovery - a sticky indicator, not the block. Only the
  undocumented bits 16 and 17 of IOMMU_RESET_REG clear it, and they reset
  units shared by all masters; the vendor's driver never writes them and
  never clears the display either (its fault handler, read from the stock
  5.4.99 kernel, invalidates at the fault address, clears the interrupt
  and resets the master bits).
- The release must not happen next to a decoding AV1 core (master 5,
  behind the same bus): before, after or during the engine's bus reset,
  guarded or not, from the error interrupt or from process context, it
  cost the AV1 stream frames or stopped the SoC until the watchdog reset
  it - in every variant tried, next to the damaged sets. Without the
  release the engine stays dead (the control arm), and on the kernel
  before this patch the AV1 stream next to the damaged sets is untouched
  only because the engine is dead from the first fault on (0 to 1 faults,
  840 to 888 bit-reader timeouts per run).

So the IOMMU driver exports sun50i_iommu_reset_client(), which pulses
the master bits of the client's fwspec, nothing more, under the IOMMU
lock. cedrus_recover() runs it before the engine's next job after a job
that ended in error (error interrupt, failed setup, watchdog): inside
the guard of 0305, while the engine's bus reset is held - nothing of
the engine is on the bus then - and only while no AV1 stream is open.
The AV1 driver tells the guard about its streams
(sunxi_ve_reset_stream_begin/end, with the core's power); while one is
open the engine is reset but its ports are left alone, and the engine
lives with them blocked until the stream closes. The H.265 bit reader
gives up at once when it times out, so a job with a blocked port ends
without further register access. Without a sun50i IOMMU the release is
a stub. Valid streams never take these paths, so nothing changes for
them.
